## Why the Previous Attempt Failed

With uniform values (all 1.0s), every thread in every block computes the same local_sum after the first phase. The shared memory reduction sums: shared_mem[tid] = blockDim.x * 1.0. Without the barrier inside the loop, threads proceed asynchronously through iterations. In iteration 1, thread 0 reads shared_mem[256] before thread 256 has written to it, getting stale data. This causes incorrect intermediate values that propagate through subsequent iterations, resulting in wrong final sums even though all threads eventually compute the same incorrect value. For the reduction loop with s = 512, 256, and so on, each thread attempts to add shared_mem[tid + s] to shared_mem[tid]. Without __syncthreads(), this creates a race condition where threads read and write to shared memory without coordination. However, the race might not always corrupt results—if warps execute in a predictable pattern or happen to schedule similarly across iterations, the computation could still produce correct or near-correct values. With uniform input values like all 1.0s, even if some reads occur before writes complete, the accumulated errors might be small enough that the final result remains close to the expected value.

