Using random values with wide variance would make the reduction more sensitive to timing errors, since any race-induced corruption would propagate through aggregation. Alternatively, very large values like 1e6 would produce a massive norm (around 2.6e11) and normalized outputs around 4, making deviations from the correct result more apparent.

The uniform 1.0 test should have caught synchronization issues if they existed, but warp-level SIMD execution and memory coherence timing might mask problems—uniform values can produce correct results even with imperfect synchronization. I should try extreme value patterns instead, concentrating large values in specific positions to maximize sensitivity to reduction order errors.

If I place mostly 1.0s with a single 1e8 value, any race condition would cause catastrophic failures: the large contribution could be added multiple times, skipped entirely, or partially included. I'll construct a test with extreme values concentrated at specific tensor positions to expose synchronization bugs that uniform patterns hide.

