But I need to be more strategic. Distributing large values across positions that map to different threads within the reduction pattern would create multiple high-magnitude contributions that must synchronize correctly.

In a 1024-thread block, the reduction proceeds in stages: threads 0-511 read from positions 512-1023, then threads 0-255 read from 256-511, and so on. If I place extreme values at indices that threads 512-1023 process initially, those threads write to shared memory, and threads 0-511 must read those values without a synchronization barrier. This creates a race condition where the read might happen before the write completes.

The strategy is to construct inputs where different threads accumulate vastly different local_sum values before the reduction begins. I can do this by placing large values at memory positions that map to high-index threads during the initial load phase, forcing the reduction to handle extreme magnitude differences and making the synchronization bug more likely to manifest.

